# Navigation and Back-Control Rules

## Status

**Decided by the owner. This is settled — do not relitigate.** Every screen must offer a way back,
and it must be the **same on-brand control everywhere** — not a bespoke pill, text link, or arrow an
agent invents for one screen. This has been shipped wrong more than once (a stray "Back to admin"
pill on the AI Review Dashboard, a stacked back-bar, a screen with no back at all), so it is now a
hard rule. Read this before adding, moving, or "fixing" any back button or screen header.

## The one shared way (mandatory)

There are exactly two shared pieces. Use them; never hand-roll a back control.

1. **`MobileScreenHeader`** (`components/shared/mobile-screen-header.tsx`) — the standard top bar:
   an accent-tinted rounded-square **back chevron** on the far left, the plugin's **brand icon**, the
   **screen title**, and the shared **report-bug / settings / account** cluster (`MobileTopActions`)
   on the right. It **self-gates on the breakpoint**: at phone width it renders the full bar; on
   desktop it renders only a slim back bar (or nothing when `desktopBack={false}`). Drop it in
   unconditionally and let it pick the right bar.
2. **`PluginRailFooter`** (`components/shared/plugin-rail-footer.tsx`) — the bottom of a plugin's
   **desktop left icon rail**: back-to-apps, account & settings, and the account menu. A screen that
   has a desktop icon rail already gets its desktop back control from this footer.

Both resolve their destination through **`resolveBackTarget(pathname)`** (`lib/nav/back-target.ts`) —
the single one-level-up policy (an `/admin/*` page → `/admin`; `/admin` → home; `/account/*` →
`/account`; everything else → all apps). **Never compute a back destination by hand** and never call
`router.back()` for the in-app back control (that is browser history, not the tailored one-level-up).

## How to wire it (by screen shape)

- **Rail-less screen (most admin shells, simple pages):** render `<MobileScreenHeader title=… accent=…
  icon=… />` as the first element. It carries the back control on both breakpoints. Done. Follow a
  shipped sibling such as `socket-relay-admin-shell.tsx` or `bug-reports-admin-shell.tsx`.
- **Screen with a desktop left icon rail (e.g. the AI Review Dashboard, community shell):** the rail's
  `PluginRailFooter` is the desktop back, and the rail is hidden at phone width — so render
  `<MobileScreenHeader … desktopBack={false} />` so the mobile header appears on phones and nothing
  duplicates on desktop. When the screen is a full-height flex/grid, wrap it so the sticky header sits
  above the content and the panes fill the remaining space (`height: 100%` of a `flex: 1` parent, not a
  raw `100vh`/`100dvh` that would overflow by the header's height). Follow `contributions-shell.tsx`.
- **Android (React Native):** the app already shows a persistent top navigator on every screen, so a
  member always has a way back. Do not add a second bespoke back control there; keep parity by relying
  on that navigator.

## Forbidden (never ship these)

- A hand-rolled back affordance: a custom "Back to …" pill/button, a bare arrow, a text link, or a raw
  `<Link href="/admin">` styled as a back control, when `MobileScreenHeader` / `PluginRailFooter`
  already cover the screen.
- A screen with **no** way back at phone width (the icon rail is hidden on mobile — that is exactly
  when the shared mobile header is required).
- A second, stacked, or duplicated back control (e.g. a mobile header **and** a bespoke pill; a desktop
  rail back **and** a desktop `MobileScreenHeader` back — use `desktopBack={false}` to opt out).
- Inventing the back destination or label inline instead of `resolveBackTarget`.

## Why this exists

Members and admins are non-technical and use the app mostly on a phone (often installed to the home
screen, where there is no browser back button). A screen with no back, or with a different-looking
back on every screen, strands them. One shared control in one place means the way back is always in
the same spot and looks the same everywhere — and an agent never has to design a back button again.
